# 1. Introduction

**Draft.** Numbers come from the files named in `data/frame-numbers.json` (run `{{run_json}}`, hls-est
`{{run_hlsest_git}}`, generated {{run_generated}}). VITIS_NOTES are cited as N*n*, OPEN_ISSUES as O*n* and
decisions as MEMOS #*n*; all are in `hls-doc/notes/`.

## 1.1 The problem: the decisions of an HLS tool are closed

High-level synthesis lets a designer write an FPGA accelerator in C or C++ and steer it with pragmas
(`pipeline`, `unroll`, `array_partition`, `bind_storage`, `dataflow`, `interface`). Surveys find that HLS
gives up some quality of results against hand-written RTL but saves a large share of development time
[@lahti2019; @cong2022fpga]. The price is that much of the hardware is decided by the tool, not by the
designer: whether a loop the designer did not annotate is pipelined, unrolled or flattened; whether an
array that is indexed by constants is split into registers; how many copies of a memory are built so that
a pipelined loop can read it several times per cycle; whether a small array goes to LUT RAM or block RAM;
and which aspect ratio each block RAM uses. AMD Vitis HLS [@amd2025ug1399], the most widely used tool,
documents its pragmas and some of its defaults, but not the rules behind these automatic decisions. Its
front end is open source [@xilinx_hlsllvm]; the optimisation passes after it, the scheduler, the binder
and the RTL generator are not.

A designer therefore learns the cost of a design only after running synthesis, which takes from seconds to
hours per design (the median C synthesis of the catalogue used here takes {{time_vitis_median}} s, and
{{vitis_timeout}} designs did not finish within the harness's time limit). Design-space exploration,
early feasibility checks and teaching all suffer from this, and so does every research tool that wants
to reason about what Vitis *will* do with a source file.

## 1.2 Why block RAM, and why per memory

This thesis concentrates on one resource, the 18 Kbit block RAM (BRAM_18K) of AMD 7-series devices, for
three reasons.

- **It is often the binding resource.** On the small Zynq-7000 part used here ({{vitis_parts}}), BRAM
  has the highest utilisation of the four resource classes (BRAM, DSP, FF, LUT) in {{util_bram_highest}}
  of the {{util_designs}} designs with a Vitis utilisation report, and {{util_bram_over100}} designs need
  more BRAM than the part has.
- **It is decided by many interacting rules.** The BRAM of one array depends on the front end (dead-array
  removal, constant folding, struct splitting), on the loop decisions (pipelining, unrolling, flattening),
  on partitioning (user pragmas and the tool's automatic partitions), on the schedule (how many accesses
  fall into one cycle), on the storage choice (LUT RAM below a size threshold, N5), on replication of
  read-heavy memories (N4, N38) and on the mapping of a bank onto RAMB18 aspect modes (N6, N8). Getting
  the BRAM of one array right means getting each of these decisions right for that array.
- **It is countable exactly.** Vitis reports BRAM_18K per memory in its synthesis report; a prediction is
  either equal or not, which makes the claim falsifiable per array.

Matching only design totals is not enough: totals can match by cancellation, an over-count in one array
hiding an under-count in another (this happened during the project, for example on PolyBench `atax`,
`bicg` and `mvt` before sequential dual-port use was modelled, O3). hls-est is therefore compared with
Vitis per memory, and a change is kept only if the designs it changes are explained per array
(the working loop in `CLAUDE.md`, rule 4).

## 1.3 Approach

The approach is behavioural black-box inference, in the spirit of measuring closed processors with
micro-benchmarks [@abel2019uops] and of model learning for black-box systems [@vaandrager2017model]:

1. Run Vitis HLS {{vitis_versions}} on a large catalogue of real designs (the HLSFactory catalogue
   [@abikaram2024hlsfactory], {{designs}} designs in {{datasets}} datasets) and record, per design, the
   memory and loop tables, the transformation log and the intermediate LLVM IR files Vitis writes.
2. Where a prediction differs from Vitis, find the first stage where Vitis and hls-est diverge, and prove
   the behaviour with a minimal kernel (often a sweep over one parameter across a suspected threshold),
   synthesised with the same configuration.
3. Write the rule down as a numbered note with its kernels and evidence paths, marking anything not
   proven as unconfirmed, and implement it as a general mechanism (never a branch on a benchmark, an
   array name or a size).
4. Re-run the whole catalogue and keep the change only if no exact design is lost without a per-array
   explanation.

The method uses only what Vitis documents or writes as output, its own `-help` text, the
open-source front end and, for device facts, a read-only look at the installed device database
(MEMOS #42). Closed binaries are never disassembled or decompiled (Chapter 8 discusses this choice).

## 1.4 Contributions

1. **A catalogue of Vitis HLS decision rules inferred by black-box experiments.** {{n_notes}} numbered
   notes (N1 to N{{n_last}}): {{n_notes_confirmed}} marked confirmed, {{n_notes_partly}} partly confirmed,
   {{n_notes_unconfirmed}} unconfirmed and {{n_notes_other}} notes on measurement method. Each note names
   its kernels and run directories, for example the automatic pipeline / unroll / flatten decision
   (N3, {{N3.kernels}} kernels), RAM_1WNR replication (N4, {{N4.kernels}} kernels; N38), the BRAM count per
   bank from RAMB18 aspect modes (N6, {{N6.kernels}} kernels) and its reconciliation with the open-source
   resource formula (N8), the partitions that remain with throughput-driven partitioning off (N7,
   {{N7.kernels}} kernels), Vitis' automatic inlining rule (N18, {{N18.kernels}} kernels) and the
   dataflow ping-pong buffers (N33, {{N33.kernels}} kernels).
2. **hls-est, an open implementation of those rules in MLIR/CIRCT** [@lattner2021mlir; @circt]: a
   {{pipeline_passes}}-pass pipeline ({{pipeline_own_passes}} of the passes written or modified for this
   work) that mirrors Vitis' stages (front-end deletion and folding, loop decisions, partitioning and
   width narrowing, modulo scheduling, storage and BRAM pricing) and explains every number in a
   machine-readable debug report that is proven not to change any estimate (Chapter 5).
3. **A C/C++ front end that compiles the real Vitis headers.** The Polygeist front end cgeist
   [@moses2021polygeist] was extended to understand the Xilinx front-end extensions (`bitwidth(N)`
   integers, bit builtins, `__fpga_*` intrinsics) and to copy Vitis' front-end behaviours (partition pragma
   merging, automatic inlining, `inline off`). Its output is checked by differential execution against
   native builds with the real headers: {{sem_equal}} of the {{sem_checked}} designs that could be checked
   are bit-exact.
4. **A validation infrastructure with per-array ground truth.** {{vitis_ok}} successful Vitis syntheses
   with {{vitis_memory_rows}} memory-table rows and {{vitis_loop_rows}} loop-table rows, a harness that
   joins hls-est and Vitis per array, and a regression net of lit tests that pins the output of every
   catalogue design ({{mirror_tests}} generated Mirror tests and {{lit_passes}} hand-written pass tests).
5. **An evaluation on the full catalogue.** Of {{compared}} compared designs, {{exact}} ({{exact_pct}} %)
   are exact and {{in_goal}} ({{in_goal_pct}} %) are exact or over by at most 15 %, at a median of
   {{time_hlsest_median}} s per design; on the six-dataset harness subset {{subset_exact}} of
   {{subset_compared}} are exact (Chapter 6). Every remaining mismatch is attributed per array to an open
   issue or listed as unexplained.
6. **A hardware-agnostic configuration and plugin architecture.** {{config_keys}} configuration keys and
   {{plugin_interfaces}} plugin interfaces (storage selection, memory architecture, memory mapping,
   pipelining and partition policies, fold rules, and the scheduling interfaces) separate the AMD device
   and the Vitis policies from the analysis, with a worked, explicitly unvalidated retargeting to
   Microchip PolarFire.
7. **An RTL back end as an application.** hls-est's static decisions are lowered directly to Verilog
   with Vitis' block-level protocol and checked by co-simulation against each design's own C testbench:
   {{rtl_verilog}} catalogue designs get Verilog and {{rtl_cosim_pass}} pass, with {{rtl_wrong_code}}
   wrong-code mismatches (Chapter 7).

## 1.5 Scope

The ground truth is one tool version, one device and one clock ({{vitis_versions}}, {{vitis_parts}},
{{vitis_clocks}}), with `syn.array_partition.throughput_driven=off`, the configuration of the original
project. Only BRAM is modelled; DSP, FF and LUT estimation (Phase 6) depend on Vitis' schedule and binding
and are future work. The success criterion, fixed at the start of the project, is that for every design with
ground truth hls-est equals Vitis or over-counts by at most 15 %; an under-count is always out of band,
because an estimate that promises a design fits when it does not is worse than a pessimistic one.

## 1.6 Outline

Chapter 2 introduces Vitis HLS and the related work on HLS estimation and open HLS compilers. Chapter 3
describes the inference method and Chapter 4 the inferred rules (separate chapters of this draft).
Chapter 5 describes the implementation. Chapter 6 evaluates hls-est against Vitis. Chapter 7 presents the
RTL back end. Chapter 8 discusses threats to validity, limitations and future work.
